WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:05.940
A new era of exploration is beginning where
space is being opened up to private citizens.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.590
Here’s a look at some of the rockets in
operation and others in development that could

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.360
take us farther than we’ve ever gone before.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.529
The Long March 5 version B is currently China’s
most powerful rocket.

00:00:16.529 --> 00:00:22.050
It stands just under 54 meters and can hold
at least 22 metric tons to low Earth orbit.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.869
This model doesn’t have a second stage.

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:27.850
Instead, there’s an extra-large payload
fairing in order to accommodate the modules

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.140
of China’s upcoming space station.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.469
The country is competing to become a dominant
player in space.

00:00:33.469 --> 00:00:38.840
There’s a long way to go to match the long
line of American launch vehicles.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.950
The Atlas V has sent spy satellites into orbit
for the U.S. military and NASA’s Perseverance

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.899
rover on a path to Mars.

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:51.579
It’s operated by United Launch Alliance (ULA),
a joint venture between Lockheed Martin and Boeing.

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:55.789
The 500 Series model, with a short payload
fairing, stands nearly 60 meters.

00:00:55.789 --> 00:01:00.239
Various configurations can accommodate up
to five solid rocket boosters.

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:04.600
The Atlas V has relied on Russian engines
to fly for nearly 20 years.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.500
That reliance will soon end when it’s replaced
by the Vulcan Centaur.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.140
ULA’s next-generation launcher uses American-made
engines.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:16.119
It’s expected to provide higher performance
than its predecessor and could make its maiden

00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:18.320
voyage in late 2021.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.457
The company has already snagged multi-billion-dollar
Pentagon launch contracts over the next few years.

00:01:23.457 --> 00:01:27.899
This version, with a standard payload fairing,
measures about 62 meters tall.

00:01:27.899 --> 00:01:31.869
The most powerful in the series is accompanied
by six solid rocket boosters.

00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:37.280
ULA says it may try to re-use the most expensive
portion of its first stage down the road - the

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.030
pair of engines - by using a heat shield to
protect them as they fall back through the

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:44.799
atmosphere before being nabbed by a helicopter
mid-air.

00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:48.840
Reusability is the key to SpaceX’s success.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.649
The famous Falcon 9 has a first-stage booster
that lands back on Earth to be used again.

00:01:53.649 --> 00:01:58.500
SpaceX’s well-known rocket has already flown
into orbit more than a hundred times, carrying

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:02.729
astronauts to the International Space Station
and launching satellites for a new internet

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:04.390
service called Starlink.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:09.060
The Falcon 9 gets its name from the nine Merlin
engines that power the first stage.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.350
Sticking three of the first stage boosters
together creates the Falcon Heavy.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:16.239
A heavy lift vehicle means it’s very powerful.

00:02:16.239 --> 00:02:21.550
This is the most powerful rocket in use - generating
more than 5 million pounds of thrust at liftoff

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:25.909
- the equivalent of 18 Boeing 747 planes.

00:02:25.909 --> 00:02:30.610
During its debut in 2018, the Falcon Heavy
launched SpaceX CEO Elon Musk’s personal

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.380
Tesla Roadster to space driven by a mannequin
named ‘Starman’.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:39.560
It can carry nearly 64 metric tons to low
Earth orbit - more than twice the payload

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:44.704
of the Delta IV Heavy which also consists
of three core boosters that make up the main body.

00:02:44.704 --> 00:02:48.670
Unlike its SpaceX counterpart, the ULA rocket
can’t be re-used.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:51.220
The first stage simply falls into the ocean.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.760
It has been operating since 2004 mainly to
launch military payloads.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.190
Retirement is around the corner.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:00.210
The final flight is expected in 2023.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.580
And as one retires, others are set to take off.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:08.720
We’re still awaiting the debut of the massive
New Glenn from Jeff Bezos’ Blue Origin.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.060
One of the reasons Bezos says he’s stepping
down as the CEO of Amazon is to focus more

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.600
on his space venture.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.590
New Glenn is the company’s first rocket
to go into orbit.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:22.090
It has a large seven-meter fairing and can send
45 metric tons to low Earth orbit.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:27.768
The heavy-lift launch vehicle will be partially
reusable. The first stage will land and fly again.

00:03:27.768 --> 00:03:32.970
The anticipated launch in 2021 has now been
delayed to late 2022 at the earliest.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:39.000
At 98 meters tall, it’s similar in height
to the initial configuration of NASA’s Space Launch System.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:43.349
The U.S. government’s flagship vehicle that’s
being built will be the most powerful rocket

00:03:43.349 --> 00:03:47.019
in its history - intended to replace the retired
Space Shuttle.

00:03:47.019 --> 00:03:51.239
The rocket uses four RS-25 engines designed
for the Shuttle program.

00:03:51.239 --> 00:03:56.200
It also has two solid rocket boosters on either
side of the huge core stage - generating nearly

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.280
nine million pounds of thrust during launch.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.924
This initial variation, Block 1, can carry
more than 27 metric tons to orbits beyond the moon.

00:04:04.924 --> 00:04:08.099
The goal is to take people to the moon and
perhaps later, to Mars.

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:11.159
However, the first launch has slipped back
multiple times.

00:04:11.159 --> 00:04:14.660
It now aims to fly uncrewed in late 2021.

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.130
The plan is for the SLS to evolve.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.300
Versions like the Block 2 Crew will carry
larger payloads farther.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:24.000
However, NASA’s suite of rockets is not
reusable.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.032
Unlike Starship.

00:04:26.032 --> 00:04:29.410
SpaceX’s fully reusable rocket under development.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:34.314
Both the Starship spacecraft and the Super
Heavy booster are designed to land and fly again.

00:04:34.314 --> 00:04:39.210
With a capability of carrying 100 metric tons
to Earth’s orbit, it will be the most powerful

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.830
rocket ever built and also the largest.

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:46.860
To give you an idea of just how big Starship
is, you can hardly see me right now unless

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.400
we zoom in really close.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:55.523
I’m 5 foot 6 or about 1.7 meters tall standing
next to this beast which is 120 meters in height.

00:04:55.523 --> 00:04:59.960
NASA has selected a modified Starship to land
the next man and first woman on the lunar

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.910
surface in a few years.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:05.910
Starship is also scheduled to fly tourists
around the moon in 2023.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:11.160
The grand goal of Elon Musk is to send people
to Mars and one day, build a city there.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:16.868
So the future of spaceflight promises to be
unlike anything humanity has ever experienced before.

